# The signature algorithm of every key this repository MINTS, per key role.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (PLAN-070 section 6.4). The algorithm used to be written
# into pkgs/rauc/gen-dev-keys.sh's openssl invocations and argued for in its
# prose, which made a later upstream upgrade a code change and made the reason
# for the choice a sentence nothing checked. It is a VALUE here instead:
# changing an algorithm is a one-line edit to this file and touches no code.
#
# Plain `KEY=value` lines only: no logic, no expansion, no command
# substitution -- the shape rootfs/scripts/ca-certificates-recorded.env and
# pkgs/rauc/versions.env already write for themselves.
#
# TOOL-NEUTRAL NAMES, not one tool's spelling. openssl wants
# `-newkey ec -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:P-256` for the two RAUC rows, and the
# package key is minted by `openssl genpkey -algorithm ED25519` rather than by
# `openssl req` at all -- a file written in openssl's request vocabulary is a
# file that cannot name its own third row. gen-dev-keys.sh maps a value to its
# tool.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE MAY NOT DECLARE, and the asymmetry is what makes a parameter
# safe: it carries VALUES, not the sets those values are checked against. A set
# is a claim about what a VERIFIER accepts and is true only because of code
# outside this repository, so making it editable beside the value would let one
# commit widen a set and adopt the new member in the same breath -- which is
# exactly the change that produces a fleet that cannot install its own updates.
# The sets, and the verifier bounding each, live in rootfs/build.sh's refusals
# A1 and A2. Changing an algorithm is editing a value; widening a set is
# editing the check and the verifier it names.
#
# THERE IS NO PER-DEPLOYMENT OVERRIDE, because the case is already served.
# Production material is PLACED in meta/ by an operator rather than minted by
# the generator, so a deployment expresses its algorithm by what it places and
# what bounds it is the set, not this default. A2 is what checks it.
#
# COMMITTED, and that is why this file is here rather than in meta/. meta/ is
# gitignored and generated when absent, so an algorithm declared only there
# would be absent from a fresh checkout, and a generator that finds no
# declaration falls back to one compiled into itself -- the hardcoded choice
# this parameter exists to remove, reintroduced one level down.

# THE RAUC CA -- meta/rauc/ca.key.pem, whose certificate is the device keyring.
# Shorter and with less to maintain than RSA, with nothing in the tree opposing
# it: RAUC verifies the bundle's CMS signature through OpenSSL, which does EC,
# and the keyring stays a file of concatenated PEMs either way, so
# docs/design/release-signing.md section 2.4's rollover and
# tests/rauc-trust-negative-test.sh's properties are untouched. The 15-year CA
# horizon is about the fleet's lifetime, not about the algorithm.
# Changing this costs a CA ROLLOVER, because a new value mints a new CA. Not a
# code change.
MOS_KEY_ALG_RAUC_CA=ecdsa-p256

# THE RAUC SIGNER -- meta/rauc/signer.key.pem, which CMS-signs every bundle.
# The determinism objection this row used to carry was measured and did not
# survive (PLAN-070 section 6.4.1): build/src/bundle.ts's rebuild gate hashes
# the squashfs payload at the head of the bundle and excludes the signature ON
# PURPOSE, and byte-identical bundles are unreachable whatever key signs them,
# because rauc salts the bundle's own dm-verity hash tree at random. The
# property the old RSA reason was protecting is not there to lose.
# Changing this costs a SIGNER REISSUE, which release-signing.md section 2.2
# already treats as cheap: it needs the CA key and no fleet update.
MOS_KEY_ALG_RAUC_SIGNER=ecdsa-p256

# THE PACKAGE SIGNING KEY -- meta/updates/root.key, lode's key over the update
# package, whose public half is baked into the manifest's trust.signingKeys.
# lode's verifier is ed25519-dalek and it accepts nothing else, so this is a
# recorded BOUND rather than a preference: `ecdsa-p256` here is not a value
# this design declines to prefer, it is a value rootfs/build.sh refuses while
# naming ed25519-dalek. It independently meets every reason the other two rows
# give -- a 32-byte public half, shorter than an uncompressed P-256 point; one
# algorithm with one curve and nothing to configure; and deterministic by
# construction, since RFC 8032 derives the nonce from the message and the key.
# There is nothing to change today: the set has one member, and it grows when
# lode's verifier does and not before.
MOS_KEY_ALG_PACKAGE=ed25519

# THE VERITY CONTENT ANCHOR -- meta/verity/signer.key.pem, whose certificate is
# compiled into every board kernel as CONFIG_SYSTEM_TRUSTED_KEYS and whose
# signature over a dm-verity root hash the kernel checks before it maps a root
# (plan 20260908-1428 section 2). The verifier here is not OpenSSL: it is the
# kernel's own PKCS#7 parser and asymmetric-key code, which is why this row
# cannot simply follow the two above it onto ecdsa-p256.
# Changing this costs a KERNEL REBUILD ON EVERY BOARD -- the anchor is not a
# file the image carries but bytes inside vmlinuz -- plus a re-signing of every
# rootfs whose signature the old key made. It is the most expensive row here.
MOS_KEY_ALG_VERITY=rsa-2048
